---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import Layout from "~/layouts/Layout.astro";
// import Nav from "~/components/Nav.astro";
import moisesLogo from "~/assets/images/logos/moises-logo.svg";

import Footer from "~/components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <!-- <Nav /> -->
  <main id="main" class="container space-y-8 md:space-y-12">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <Image src={moisesLogo} alt="Moises Logo" class="w-40" />
      <span class="block text-slate-300">&vert;</span>
      <a href="/" class="logo !text-xl" aria-label="Giles Butler home">
        Giles Butler<span class="logo-dot">.</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">
        Senior Product Designer <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span>{" "}
        Design Engineer <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Music
        Producer
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <h1>I design complex creative software and make it feel simple.</h1>

        <p>
          I have spent 15+ years designing and building digital products, and
          more than 25 years producing electronic music.
        </p>

        <p>
          My career has moved between product design, frontend and full-stack
          engineering, and design engineering. Today I work across those
          disciplines: understanding the problem, shaping the product, designing
          the interaction, and building working prototypes when code is the
          fastest way to find the answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          For the last decade I have worked in startups, building products from
          zero to one, evolving them through direct customer feedback, creating
          systems that can scale, and making new technologies such as AI
          understandable to everyday users.
        </p>

        <p>
          Music has run alongside all of it. I started producing and DJing at
          12, studied Music Production, have used Ableton since Live 4 and
          previously beta tested it, learnt Logic before the Apple acquisition,
          released music through labels, and continue to produce and release
          music from my own studio.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex gap-2 mb-6">
        <a class="btn-pill !text-white !no-underline" href="#work">
          <span class="ico">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path
                d="M5 12h13M12 6l6 6-6 6"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2.2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </span>
          <span>Explore my work</span>
        </a>
        <a class="btn-outline btn-pill !no-underline" href="/cv.pdf">
          <span class="ico rotate-90">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path
                d="M5 12h13M12 6l6 6-6 6"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2.2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </span>
          <span>Download my CV</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <ul class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 list-none">
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2 bg-slate-100 rounded-sm p-4 ring-1 ring-slate-200 font-semibold text-slate-700">
          <Icon name="lucide:globe" class="" /> 15+ years Product & technology
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2 bg-slate-100 rounded-sm p-4 ring-1 ring-slate-200 font-semibold text-slate-700">
          <Icon name="lucide:music" /> 25+ years Music production
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2 bg-slate-100 rounded-sm p-4 ring-1 ring-slate-200 font-semibold text-slate-700">
          <Icon name="lucide:rocket" /> 10+ years Startups
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2 bg-slate-100 rounded-sm p-4 ring-1 ring-slate-200 font-semibold text-slate-700">
          <Icon name="lucide:code" /> Code-first Prototyping
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Why Moises -->
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Why Moises?</h2>
      <h3>
        Music, technology and design have always been the same problem for me.
      </h3>

      <p>
        There are not many roles where the different parts of my background
        converge this directly.
      </p>

      <p>
        I have spent my professional career making technically complex software
        easier to understand and use. I have spent most of my life outside work
        making electronic music, learning instruments and tools deeply enough
        that the technology becomes part of the creative process rather than
        something I have to think about.
      </p>

      <p>That is the product problem I find most interesting.</p>

      <p>
        The best creative software can be extraordinarily powerful without
        constantly reminding you how complicated it is. It helps you move from
        an idea to a result while preserving control, authorship and the ability
        to explore.
      </p>

      <p>
        AI makes that problem more interesting. A system can now do far more on
        a musician's behalf, but greater capability also creates new interaction
        questions: what should be automated, what needs to remain explicit, how
        should uncertainty be communicated, how can changes remain
        understandable and reversible, and how do you make the technology feel
        like part of the instrument rather than a layer sitting on top of it?
      </p>

      <p>
        That intersection of music, product design, engineering and emerging
        technology is where I want to work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Work -->
    <section class="prose" id="work">
      <h2>
        I am most useful when the product is complicated underneath and needs to
        feel obvious on the surface.
      </h2>

      <p>
        My background lets me move comfortably between the experience someone
        sees and the system making it possible. I can discuss interaction and
        visual hierarchy with designers, component architecture and
        implementation constraints with engineers, product direction with
        founders and PMs, and the actual workflow with customers.
      </p>

      <p>
        I use that range to shorten the distance between an idea and something
        we can put in front of people.
      </p>

      <h3>Selected work</h3>

      <div>
        <h4>Mixo</h4>

        <h5>
          Making powerful AI usable by people who do not think of themselves as
          technical
        </h5>

        <h6>
          Product design <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Design
          engineering <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> AI{" "}
          <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> SaaS
        </h6>

        <p>
          Mixo uses AI to turn a short description of a business into a working
          website.
        </p>

        <p>
          My work has sat across product design and engineering, helping turn
          the complexity of generation, editing and publishing into an
          experience that feels immediate and approachable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Designing an AI-native product changes some of the normal assumptions
          of interface design. Outputs are probabilistic. Generation takes time.
          People need to understand what the system has done without
          understanding the technology behind it. And as the product becomes
          more capable, additional power cannot simply become additional
          interface.
        </p>

        <p>
          I have worked across those problems through product design, coded
          prototyping, frontend implementation, component systems, customer
          feedback and continuous iteration.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            "Make the first result useful quickly, then make everything
            understandable enough to change."
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Serves more than 750,000 businesses</p>
        <p>Created over 3 million websites</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h4>Mixvisor</h4>

        <h5>Designing around how music projects actually exist</h5>

        <h6>
          Founder <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Product design{" "}
          <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Engineering{" "}
          <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Music technology
        </h6>

        <p>Mixvisor started with a problem from my own studio.</p>

        <p>
          After years of producing music, sessions accumulate across folders,
          drives, versions and different DAWs. A generic filesystem knows where
          those files are, but it knows almost nothing about what they mean to a
          musician.
        </p>

        <p>
          I designed and built Mixvisor around the project as the primary object
          rather than the file.
        </p>

        <p>
          It scans existing music folders, recognises DAW sessions, creates a
          searchable project library and makes it easier to understand what you
          have been working on, where it lives and what state it is in.
        </p>

        <p>
          The interesting product problem is the gap between the computer's
          model of a filesystem and the musician's mental model of their work.
        </p>

        <p>
          That required designing around imperfect source data, multiple DAW
          formats, previewable audio, large libraries and workflows that had
          already evolved organically over years.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            "The filesystem organises files. A producer thinks in songs,
            sessions, versions and ideas."
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Mixvisor currently recognises projects from Ableton, Logic, FL Studio,
          Bitwig, Reaper, Studio One, Reason and Cubase.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h4>Room for Sound</h4>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Music -->
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>A product designer that codes. A musician that designs software.</h2>
      <p>
        I started producing and DJing when I was 12 and later completed a degree
        in Music Production.
      </p>
      <p>
        I learnt Logic while it was still an Emagic product and have used
        Ableton Live since version 4, including participating in its beta
        programme. I have released electronic music through labels, DJed, and
        recently returned to releasing independently through Bandcamp.
      </p>
      <p>
        My current practice has moved increasingly towards hardware, sampling
        and the relationship between physical instruments and software. I
        maintain my own studio and still make music because making music is what
        led me towards creative technology in the first place.
      </p>
      <img src="" alt="" />
      soundcloud release quote about process
    </section>

    <!-- Design -->
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Desgin</h2>

      <h3>Understand the actual workflow</h3>

      <p>
        I start by understanding what people are really trying to accomplish,
        not by translating feature requests directly into interfaces.
      </p>

      <p>
        That usually means talking to customers, observing existing behaviour
        and looking closely at the workarounds people have created for
        themselves. A workaround often contains more useful product information
        than a stated preference.
      </p>

      <h3>Find the expensive uncertainty</h3>

      <p>
        Before increasing fidelity, I want to know what we are uncertain about.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes that is whether the concept solves a real problem. Sometimes
        it is information architecture. Sometimes the entire question is how an
        interaction feels after 300 milliseconds.
      </p>

      <p>The prototype should match the uncertainty.</p>

      <h3>Use code when behaviour is the design</h3>

      <p>
        I spent years working professionally in frontend and full-stack
        engineering before those skills converged with design engineering.
      </p>

      <p>
        For products involving complex state, animation, responsiveness, real
        data or AI, I usually move into code early. Working software makes
        assumptions visible and creates something product, design and
        engineering can reason about together.
      </p>

      <h3>Design the system behind the screen</h3>

      <p>
        Interfaces rarely fail because an individual screen was badly drawn.
        Problems accumulate when states, components, behaviours and concepts do
        not form a coherent system.
      </p>

      <p>
        I therefore think about component architecture, interaction patterns and
        product models alongside visual design.
      </p>

      <h3>Ship to learn</h3>

      <p>
        A decade in startups has made me comfortable working before everything
        is known.
      </p>

      <p>
        I care about craft, but craft and completeness are different things. The
        useful question is how much fidelity this particular decision deserves
        before putting it in front of a real person.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Design Engineering -->
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Design x Engineering</h2>
      <h3>
        For some design questions, the shortest route to an answer is working
        software.
      </h3>
      show an animation cycling: Sketch → model → prototype → test → change →
      ship
      <p>
        I began my career as a designer, moved into frontend development and
        later full-stack engineering, then found my way back to the point where
        those disciplines overlap.
      </p>
      <p>That history affects how I work today.</p>
      <p>
        I can design with implementation constraints in mind without allowing
        the current implementation to define the solution. I can explore
        interaction directly in the browser. I can work on a design system as
        both an interface language and a component architecture. And I can
        collaborate with engineers at the level of behaviour and systems rather
        than handing over pictures of finished screens.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agentic coding has accelerated that process significantly. My current
        workflow combines code agents, structured specifications and independent
        model review so that I can move from an interaction idea to a
        functioning prototype extremely quickly while still reviewing
        architecture and output critically.
      </p>
      <button>View current prototyping stack →</button> (OpenCode / OpenSpec /
      Oh My Pi / Babysitter / local Qwen setup hidden)
    </section>

    <!-- Experience -->
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Experience</h2>

      <h3>15+ years intersecting between design, engineering and product</h3>

      <p>My career has not followed a clean boundary between disciplines.</p>

      <p>
        I started in design, moved deeper into frontend and then full-stack
        engineering, and eventually converged on design engineering and product
        work. That means I approach products with an understanding of both what
        the experience needs to become and what is required to build and evolve
        it.
      </p>

      <!-- Roles -->
      <h4>Roles</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Company / product Job title <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span>{" "}
          dates One sentence describing what the product/company actually does.
          Then only the three strongest pieces of evidence, ideally one each
          covering: product/design outcome{" "}
          <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span>
          technical/system contribution{" "}
          <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> measurable business/user
          impact Not a conventional responsibility list.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Contact -->
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>This is the kind of product work I want to work on.</h2>

      <p>
        I have spent much of my career at the intersection of design and
        engineering, and most of my life around music.
      </p>

      <p>
        Moises is one of the few places where those backgrounds are not separate
        advantages. They are parts of the same job.
      </p>

      <p>
        Email <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> LinkedIn{" "}
        <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> GitHub{" "}
        <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Bandcamp{" "}
        <span class="text-slate-300">&bull;</span> Download CV
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  :root {
    --paper: #011316;
    --line: #005e65;
  }

  .canvas-bg {
    background-size: 34px 89px;
  }
</style>

<style>
  @reference "../styles/global.css";

  body,
  h1,
  h2,
  h3,
  h4,
  h5,
  h6,
  p,
  li,
  .fluid,
  textarea,
  input,
  select,
  button,
  th,
  td {
    --fl: 0;
    --font-size-min: 14;
    --font-size-max: 20;
    --font-ratio-min: 1.25;
    --font-ratio-max: 1.3333333333333333;
    --font-width-min: 320;
    --font-width-max: 1500;
    --fluid-min: calc(
      var(--font-size-min) * pow(var(--font-ratio-min), var(--fl, 0))
    );
    --fluid-max: calc(
      var(--font-size-max) * pow(var(--font-ratio-max), var(--fl, 0))
    );
    --fluid-preferred: calc(
      (var(--fluid-max) - var(--fluid-min)) /
        (var(--font-width-max) - var(--font-width-min))
    );
    --fluid-type: clamp(
      (var(--fluid-min) / 16) * 1rem,
      ((var(--fluid-min) / 16) * 1rem) -
        (((var(--fluid-preferred) * var(--font-width-min)) / 16) * 1rem) +
        (var(--fluid-preferred) * var(--variable-unit, 100vi)),
      (var(--fluid-max) / 16) * 1rem
    );
    font-size: var(--fluid-type);
  }

  /* This makes containers, with container queries use a cqi unit instead of vi */
  .fluid-text-container {
    container-type: inline-size;
    --variable-unit: 100cqi;
  }

  h1,
  h2,
  h3,
  h4,
  h5,
  h6 {
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  h1,
  h2,
  h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    line-height: 0.86;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }

  h1,
  .h1 {
    --fl: 4;
    font-weight: 900;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  h2,
  .h2 {
    --fl: 3;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  h3,
  .h3 {
    --fl: 2;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  h4,
  .h4 {
    --fl: 1;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  h5,
  .h5 {
    --fl: 1;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  h6,
  .h6 {
    --fl: 0;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  p,
  li,
  body {
    --fl: 0;
  }
  .fs-xs {
    --fl: -1;
  }
  .fs-base {
    --fl: 0;
  }
  .fs-s {
    --fl: 1;
  }
  .fs-m {
    --fl: 2;
  }
  .fs-l {
    --fl: 3;
  }
  .fs-xl {
    --fl: 4;
  }
  .fs-xxl {
    --fl: 5;
  }
  .fs-xxxl {
    --fl: 6;
  }

  .container {
    @apply mx-auto w-full bg-white py-4 md:py-8 lg:py-16 px-4 md:px-8 lg:px-16 border-x border-slate-100 rounded-xs mt-20 md:mt-32 lg:mt-40;
    max-width: clamp(300px, 80vw, 1200px);
  }

  .prose {
    @apply px-0;
    max-width: clamp(200px, 80vw, 1200px);
  }
</style>
